1
00:00:01,280 --> 00:00:06,240
After looking at Lamport's bakery algorithm, 
let's move to a slightly different problem, that  

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:12,000
of the readers and writers. In this case, we have 
multiple entities accessing a shared resource, but  

3
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:16,880
such entities are of different types and we have 
different safety properties associated with them.

4
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:22,480
Both writers and readers are trying to access 
a shared resource, for instance, a file. 

5
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:25,600
Only one writer at the time 
can write to such a resource. 

6
00:00:26,480 --> 00:00:30,800
Multiple readers are allowed to read at the 
same time, but not concurrently with writers. 

7
00:00:31,440 --> 00:00:35,520
We are going to look at how we can solve this 
synchronization problem using semaphores.

8
00:00:36,560 --> 00:00:39,280
If we try to visualize the 
dependencies between the  

9
00:00:39,280 --> 00:00:43,920
entry and exit sections of writers 
and readers, it looks like this.

10
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:49,120
For the writer, the entry section needs to 
wait until no one accesses the shared object. 

11
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:55,040
Once the critical section is over (i.e., 
the portion in which the writer writes to  

12
00:00:55,040 --> 00:01:00,240
the shared resource), the exit section can 
send a signal about the fact that no one is  

13
00:01:00,240 --> 00:01:05,680
accessing the object anymore, and such a signal 
can be consumed by both a writer or a reader.

14
00:01:06,640 --> 00:01:10,560
For the reader, the entry section 
should first check if the reader  

15
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:14,880
is the first reader of a batch of readers 
that tries to access the shared resource.  

16
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:20,160
If that is the case, then the reader should 
wait. If other readers are already accessing  

17
00:01:20,160 --> 00:01:23,200
the resource, then the reader can 
simply enter its critical section. 

18
00:01:23,920 --> 00:01:28,320
For the exit section, the reader should of 
course inform others it is done with its  

19
00:01:28,320 --> 00:01:34,880
critical section. If the reader was the only one / 
the last one completing its critical section, then  

20
00:01:34,880 --> 00:01:40,400
a signal should be sent for writers or for another 
batch of readers to enter their critical section.

21
00:01:42,000 --> 00:01:46,640
Let's now look at the pseudocode that uses 
semaphores according to these dependencies.

22
00:01:47,280 --> 00:01:50,640
We have writers and readers 
sharing the following variables: 

23
00:01:50,640 --> 00:01:58,080
- two binary semaphores, noone_accesses and 
protect_check (notice that both are initially 1),  

24
00:01:58,080 --> 00:01:59,280
and
- rc,  

25
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,840
readers counter, which is initially set to 0.

26
00:02:02,560 --> 00:02:07,840
The writers simply protect their critical 
section by waiting on the noone_accesses variable  

27
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,200
(entry section) and sending a signal 
on the same variable (exit section).

28
00:02:14,160 --> 00:02:20,160
The readers use the protect_check variable to 
make sure they consistently update the rc value. 

29
00:02:20,880 --> 00:02:24,240
In the entry section, the first reader 
that manages to increase it to 1 (i.e.,  

30
00:02:24,880 --> 00:02:27,440
the first reader of a batch) also waits on  

31
00:02:27,440 --> 00:02:30,640
the noone_accesses variable (to 
synchronize with the writers). 

32
00:02:31,760 --> 00:02:38,880
In the exit section, the reader that sets rc 
to 0 (notice access to rc is also synchronized  

33
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:44,800
here using the protect_check variable among 
readers) also signals on the noone_accesses  

34
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:49,840
variable, so that a writer or another batch of 
readers can then enter the critical section.

35
00:02:50,800 --> 00:02:57,200
As before, I suggest you try to have some formal 
reasoning about how this solution is correct,  

36
00:02:57,200 --> 00:02:59,760
at least in terms of mutual 
exclusion and progress. 

37
00:03:00,480 --> 00:03:06,240
If you think about fairness, you can probably see 
that here readers are privileged over writers.  

38
00:03:06,240 --> 00:03:12,320
Once a reader increases rc to 1, other readers can 
keep accessing their critical section an unbounded  

39
00:03:12,320 --> 00:03:19,520
number of times. As long as rc is not set back 
to 0, writers cannot access the shared resource.

